 obj:[["Introduce yourself and other people with am, is and are.","تقديم نفسك والآخرين باستخدام am و is و are."],["Make negatives with not.","تكوين النفي باستخدام not."],["Ask and answer yes / no questions with to be.","طرح أسئلة نعم / لا والإجابة عنها بفعل to be."]],
 en:["The verb to be has three forms in the present: am, is and are. We use it to say who we are, what our job is, where we are from and how old we are.","Use am with I, is with he, she and it, and are with you, we and they. In speaking we usually use the short forms: I'm, she's, we're.","To make a negative, add not after the verb: I'm not, he isn't, they aren't. To make a question, put the verb before the subject: Are you new here?","Arabic often has no verb in sentences like these, so learners forget it. In English the verb must always be there: \"She is an accountant\", never \"She accountant\"."],
 ar:["فعل to be له ثلاث صور في المضارع: am و is و are. نستخدمه لنقول من نحن، وما وظيفتنا، ومن أين نحن، وكم عمرنا.","نستخدم am مع I، و is مع he و she و it، و are مع you و we و they. وفي الكلام نستخدم غالبًا الصيغ المختصرة: I'm و she's و we're.","للنفي نضيف not بعد الفعل: I'm not و he isn't و they aren't. وللسؤال نقدّم الفعل على الفاعل: Are you new here?","الجملة العربية في مثل هذه الحالات لا تحتاج فعلًا، ولذلك ينساه المتعلم. أما في الإنجليزية فالفعل لازم دائمًا: She is an accountant وليس She accountant."],
 forms:[
 ["Affirmative","الإثبات","I am · He / She / It is · You / We / They are","I'm from Cairo, in Egypt.","We're in the same department."],
 ["Negative","النفي","Subject + am not / isn't / aren't","I'm not a teacher.","No, it isn't."],
 ["Question","السؤال","Am / Is / Are + subject ?","Are you new here?","Is your office near here?"]
 ],
