 en:["We use the past continuous for an action that was in progress at a certain time in the past: \"At nine o'clock I was driving home.\" It answers the question \"What were you doing?\"","The form is was or were + verb-ing: I was watching, she was cooking, they were doing their homework. For the negative, add not: wasn't, weren't. For a question, put was or were first.","It often works together with the past simple. The past continuous is the long background action; the past simple is the short action that interrupts it: \"I was watching a match when the lights went out.\"","Use when before the short action, and while before the long one: \"While I was waiting, I saw an accident.\" In stories, the past continuous also sets the scene: \"It was raining. People were standing in the street.\""],
 ar:["نستخدم الماضي المستمر لفعل كان جاريًا في وقت معين في الماضي: At nine o'clock I was driving home. وهو يجيب عن السؤال What were you doing?","التركيب: was أو were + الفعل مضافًا إليه ing: I was watching و she was cooking و they were doing their homework. وللنفي نضيف not: wasn't و weren't. وللسؤال نقدّم was أو were.","يأتي كثيرًا مع الماضي البسيط. الماضي المستمر هو الفعل الطويل في الخلفية، والماضي البسيط هو الفعل القصير الذي يقطعه: I was watching a match when the lights went out.","نستخدم when قبل الفعل القصير، و while قبل الفعل الطويل: While I was waiting, I saw an accident. وفي الحكايات يرسم الماضي المستمر المشهد: It was raining. People were standing in the street."],
