 obj:[["Describe how someone does something.","وصف الطريقة التي يفعل بها شخص شيئًا."],["Form adverbs with -ly and learn the irregular ones.","تكوين الظروف بـ ly وحفظ الشاذ منها."],["Put the adverb in the right place.","وضع الظرف في مكانه الصحيح."]],
 en:["An adverb of manner tells us how an action is done: \"She speaks clearly\", \"He drives carefully\". It describes the verb. An adjective describes a noun: \"She is a careful driver.\"","Most adverbs are adjective + -ly: quick → quickly, clear → clearly, polite → politely. If the adjective ends in -y, change y to i: easy → easily. If it ends in -ful, you get -fully: careful → carefully.","A few are irregular. Good becomes well. Fast, hard and late do not change: \"He works hard\", \"She drives fast\". Be careful: hardly does not mean \"with effort\"; it means \"almost not\".","The adverb comes after the verb, or after the object if there is one. Never put it between the verb and its object: say \"She speaks English well\", not \"She speaks well English\"."],
 ar:["ظرف الحال يبيّن كيف يتم الفعل: She speaks clearly و He drives carefully. فهو يصف الفعل. أما الصفة فتصف الاسم: She is a careful driver.","معظم الظروف تتكون من الصفة + ly: quick ← quickly و clear ← clearly و polite ← politely. وإذا انتهت الصفة بـ y تتحول إلى i: easy ← easily. وإذا انتهت بـ ful تصبح fully: careful ← carefully.","بعضها شاذ. good تصبح well. و fast و hard و late لا تتغير: He works hard و She drives fast. وانتبه: hardly لا تعني «بجدّ»، بل تعني «بالكاد».","يأتي الظرف بعد الفعل، أو بعد المفعول إن وُجد. ولا نضعه أبدًا بين الفعل ومفعوله: نقول She speaks English well وليس She speaks well English."],
